1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,500
A few people ask me what you're actually meant to do with Scout when your team's on defense or getting completely rolled or you're playing on some narrow map like Dustbolt where there's barely anywhere to move and they all sound like different questions but it's pretty much the same problem. You can't just hold W and take whatever fight you want anymore because Scout feels ridiculous when your team's doing well. Everyone's distracted, people are getting separated, you've got space to run around and you're usually the first person there whenever a fight opens up but the second your team loses all that pressure and every doorway starts getting watched,

2
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:35,000
Scout can go from feeling like he has no limitations to feeling like one of the most useless classes in the game.

3
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:42,560
So a lot of this video is gonna be about slowing down a bit, using chip damage,

4
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:50,740
sitting further back on high ground where you can actually see what's going on, and waiting for someone to overextend instead of launching yourself into three people every time you see their backs turned.

5
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:53,380
Because even when an opening looks really good,

6
00:00:53,380 --> 00:00:55,440
you still have to think about whether your team is alive,

7
00:00:55,440 --> 00:01:13,820
Whether anyone can actually help you and whether dying for that play is even worth it, especially when you're defending on red in payload or attack defend because you can't take your life for granted like blue team can because those respawn times on certain maps get just ridiculous and one bad death can take you out of the fight for what feels like forever.

8
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:27,360
And Scout can create way more for his team than the scoreboard actually shows. You can make half the enemy team turn around, pull someone away from the objective, or force their medic backwards, but your team still has to notice and do something with it.

9
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:41,600
So we're going to go through all of that with some pre-recorded clips that I'm going to lightly commentate, and how to stay useful when you can't hold W, how to know when to commit, and how to tell when you made a bad play versus when your team just didn't take advantage of what you created.

10
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:48,220
In downtime situations like this on defense, chip damage is sometimes all you can really do, but it's more important than people think.

11
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:53,920
It makes people look back, slows them down, and forces them to look for heals. And make sure not just when pestering people,

12
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:58,100
but in general, make sure you're on an elevated platform when holding any position with scout.

13
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:02,380
It's crucial that if you get the chance to not let engineers get their sentry built in the first place.

14
00:02:02,380 --> 00:02:06,480
If it's level 3, you alert your team and when your combo comes out to deal with it,

15
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:10,100
help them if you can at that very moment, but otherwise pay attention to the flanks.

16
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:16,480
The engineer was on the ball here and he starts building again, so I call it out to my soldier friend and he deals with it straight away.

17
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:22,700
The engineer is probably thinking, oh my god, I'm so unlucky. They keep walking out soon as I build my stuff.

18
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:25,520
But it isn't luck. I'm scouting out these areas.

19
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:39,340
I being an actual scout and I communicating what I say to my team At the same time I not overextending I treating these two flanks like they my post I contesting them and I cleaning up anyone who tries to walk through But I not running forward trying to take a 1v2

20
00:02:39,340 --> 00:02:45,500
I'm just taking it slow, taking my time and making sure these areas stay contested because after all we're playing defense.

21
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:50,180
We're not the ones attacking. You don't need to hold W as much as you might think just because you're playing scout.

22
00:02:59,260 --> 00:03:03,380
Oh fuck's sake.

23
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:29,240
Right here I'm backed into a corner by a pyro and a scout. It doesn't look like I have anywhere to go but there's this tiny slope in the ground. I run up it, jump backwards and it gives me just enough height to double jump forward over the pyro's head. That tiny bit of elevation saves me from getting flamed and just happens to set me up to kill the scout as well. That's one of the best skills you can have a scout, not just seamlessly using anything around you but identifying what can be used as high ground in the first place.

24
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:44,260
It's a bit like Emmet becoming a master builder at the end of the Lego movie, except you're looking at every box, railing or tiny slope and thinking, can I use it as high ground? Basically any type of elevator platform will let you get extra height and reposition with less risk.

25
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:52,260
Like the last clip, this is a defensive situation that isn't as straightforward as holding a boxed in last with two obvious flanks.

26
00:03:52,260 --> 00:04:00,260
When you're forced to fight in more open areas without much pressure from your team, the scout needs to take some of that pressure off them by becoming the centre of attention without just throwing his life away.

27
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:08,260
It's about occupying spaces the enemy can't ignore, forcing them to deal with you and drawing attention away from your team while still prioritising survivability.

28
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:20,360
Once your team is no longer occupying the space you were trying to defend and the enemy

29
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:24,280
has taken full control of that area, your presence there becomes much less valuable.

30
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:27,580
If you still have space behind you to retreat into, use it.

31
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:31,620
There's no reason to keep testing an area your team no longer has any presence in, especially

32
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:32,900
when you're on defense.

33
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:37,860
Your job isn't to take the space back by yourself, it's to stay alive, continue slowing them down,

34
00:04:37,860 --> 00:04:46,860
Keep running time off the clock, but you need to be alive to do this. Where blue team is all about trying to get one big coordinated push to break through, red is more about survivability.

35
00:04:53,860 --> 00:05:20,519
The previous clip was a good example of what Scout should be doing when his team doesn have much presence in the area but when your team is established and actually occupying the space you defending your role changes Now your job is to prevent threats from reaching your team by pestering them or killing them like this sniper I focus in on to prevent him landing an easy headshot on my heavy or med And clean up easy picks on enemies your power class is already engaging with and safely apply chip damage that forces blue to retreat or delays their push

36
00:05:20,519 --> 00:05:27,519
You're still trying to have as much impact as possible, but without over committing and throwing away the position your team has already established.

37
00:05:33,519 --> 00:05:51,979
Another question I got was how you're meant to play scout on narrow maps like Dust Bowl.

38
00:05:51,979 --> 00:05:55,479
Honestly, maps like Dust Bowl and Gold Rush are where you need to take all of this advice

39
00:05:55,479 --> 00:06:00,819
the most seriously. They're old restrictive maps where the balance has aged pretty badly,

40
00:06:00,819 --> 00:06:07,019
Especially for red. Your respawn could be anywhere from 18 to 22 seconds and the points can be captured absurdly quickly.

41
00:06:07,019 --> 00:06:11,879
And because of that there isn't some secret flank or defensive strategy that suddenly makes Scout amazing.

42
00:06:11,879 --> 00:06:18,179
You have to be extremely deliberate about where you position yourself, which fights you take and whether you are actually where your team needs you.

43
00:06:18,339 --> 00:06:26,699
High ground is still important, but only if it's actually safe. Most of the obvious high ground on the first section of Dust Bowl leaves you exposed to snipers and spam.

44
00:06:26,699 --> 00:06:32,699
Sometimes you genuinely have to play around the point and be prepared to back up once your team loses that space.

45
00:06:32,699 --> 00:06:39,699
In this clip I move around and I help my team defend the choke, but I always end up returning to the same flank besides the point.

46
00:06:39,699 --> 00:06:44,699
It gives me cover, close range fights and an easy escape, while still allowing me to directly defend the point.

47
00:06:44,699 --> 00:06:50,699
It's also a really common place for blue to put a teleporter, so it's naturally somewhere Scout can find a lot of value.

48
00:06:50,699 --> 00:06:58,779
Target priority is also important. In general, if you see something like a direct hit soldier, there's a good chance he's using it because he wants to destroy your team's sentry nest.

49
00:06:59,339 --> 00:07:04,499
Scout can choose these fights, so you should use that freedom to focus the people who are most threatening to your team.

50
00:07:04,959 --> 00:07:08,479
You need to play as a team better than Blue does on old maps like Gold Rush and Dust Bowl.

51
00:07:08,859 --> 00:07:14,399
Something I like doing is briefly moving into Blue's territory and getting someone's attention and then immediately retreating.

52
00:07:14,819 --> 00:07:19,379
Impatient players will often overreact and chase you too far, which brings them directly into your team.

53
00:07:19,379 --> 00:07:28,279
That's what happens to this pyro when he follows me up the stairs just then and my soldier friend already was holding that position behind me and I knew that so he deals with the pyro straight away.

54
00:07:28,279 --> 00:07:33,279
I'm not just running away from the fight, I'm dragging the pyro into a fight that's much better for my team.

55
00:07:33,279 --> 00:07:47,639
And give coldouts a scout because I already mentioned this but he cold scout for a reason and you often the first person who can see what Blue is doing So make your team aware of anything they deserve to know about It doesn need to be complicated

56
00:07:48,379 --> 00:07:54,659
You know it's just that their combo is coming through the top left flank or a pyro is jetpacking towards you or someone is moving into your team space.

57
00:07:54,659 --> 00:07:58,979
Even basic information just gives your team time to react and capitalise.

58
00:08:00,419 --> 00:08:04,799
Now I know not everyone wants to treat casual like a scrim and constantly give out cold outs.

59
00:08:04,799 --> 00:08:08,359
But this sixes clip is the clearest way I can show you how powerful they are.

60
00:08:08,719 --> 00:08:13,219
And how powerful Scalper comes when his team can actually capitalise on the openings he creates.

61
00:08:13,959 --> 00:08:15,919
I don't create this opening by dropping the medic.

62
00:08:16,139 --> 00:08:20,119
I create it by forcing basically their entire team to turn around and focus on me.

63
00:08:20,439 --> 00:08:22,659
For those few seconds none of them are looking at my team.

64
00:08:22,759 --> 00:08:26,939
Which gives everyone else the opportunity to move forward and wipe out basically their entire team.

65
00:08:27,239 --> 00:08:29,619
But that's only powerful if my team knows what's happening.

66
00:08:29,619 --> 00:08:35,699
I can't expect them to capitalize on the entire enemy team looking at me unless I call it out and make them aware.

67
00:08:36,219 --> 00:08:40,839
Once I do, they know that the opening exists and can immediately take advantage of it.

68
00:08:41,059 --> 00:08:45,219
And this is what it looks like in full swing when your team capitalizes on the opening Scout creates.

69
00:08:45,579 --> 00:08:53,059
Your value isn't always the player you kill, sometimes it's how much attention you pull away from everyone else, and this absolutely applies to casual as well.

70
00:08:53,479 --> 00:08:56,379
People just don't communicate it nearly enough, especially a Scout.

71
